e sits above its DCF fair value of $3.80 (margin of safety -39%), so there is no valuation cushion.","The fair-value models corroborate one another (dispersion 7%), and the Monte Carlo range runs $2.45 to $6.31 (P10-P90).","Multiples send mixed signals against Real Estate peers: cheaper on EV/EBITDA 11.7 vs 16.2, richer on P/E 88.3 vs 23.9 and P/FCF 30.4 vs 17.8 (sector median).","Against its own history it is cheap: the EBIT multiple sits 46% below its 10-year median.","Earnings yields are thin: an FCF yield of 3.3% (below the risk-free rate) and an EBIT/EV of 3.7% (vs the 8% bar).","The 5 covering analysts target $5.50 on average, 4% above the price, well away from the model's fair value."],"prof":["ROIC of 20% clears the cost of capital of 9.4% by 10pp, the mark of a value creator; returns have swung a lot from year to year (CV 0.34).","It converts 7% of revenue into free cash flow, or 3% after stock compensation.","AFFO margin (a proxy for distributable REIT cash) is 7%.","ROE of 4% comes mostly from margins and asset turnover rather than leverage (4.2x equity multiplier).","It spends 3.3% of revenue on stock compensation (above the 2% gate)."],"hlth":["The company holds more cash than debt.","Warning signs: an Altman Z of 0.61 in the distress zone."],"growth":["Revenue has compounded at 1.9% over 3 years, 4.7% over 5 years and 1.6% over 10 years, so growth is decelerating, and it has been lumpy (year-to-year swings of 57%).","At current returns, reinvesting 3% of profits supports about 0.6% a year of fundamental growth, too little to drive meaningful growth.","Free cash flow has compounded -8.4% a year over five years, short of the 5% durability bar.","Forward indicators: FFO growth of 6.4% a year over five years.","Capex roughly matches depreciation (1.0x, 25.8% of sales), a maintenance pace.","Its Rule of 40 score is 8, below the 40 that marks a healthy growth business."],"own":["Insiders own 8.4%, meaningful skin in the game, and reported institutional holdi